
### The Summit Price

The number stared back at Arjun from the government website, cold and digital: $11,000. Not for a car, or a house, or a lifetime of savings. This was the price for a single piece of paper. A permit. Permission for a team of seven foreigners to try to stand on the summit of his ancestral mountain.

Arjun’s family had lived in the shadow of the peak for generations. They called it *Ama-la*, Mother Mountain. To them, its value was in the water that cascaded from its glaciers, feeding their barley fields. Its price was the risk taken by his grandfather, who guided early British explorers for a few rupees and a tin of biscuits, his navigation skills written off as "local intuition."

Now, Arjun sat in his small office in Kathmandu, the owner of a fledgling trekking and expedition company. The $11,000 was just the permit fee paid to the government. It didn't include the Liaison Officer's salary, the garbage deposit, the insurance, the oxygen, the food, the Sherpa bonuses. For the clients, the total cost would be closer to $50,000 each. For Arjun, the permit was the gatekeeper, the first and most formidable hurdle before a single rope was even coiled.

His clients, led by a bullish American named Mark, saw it as a transaction. An entry ticket. "It's the cost of doing business on an 8000-meter peak, Arjun!" Mark had boomed over a satellite phone. "Just factor it in. The guys who want to come, they can afford it."

But Arjun couldn't just "factor it in." He thought of his friend, Pasang, a stronger climber than any of the foreigners on their team. Pasang, who knew the mountain's every mood, who could smell a weather window change on the wind. Pasang could never afford this permit. The mountain of his birth was priced out of his reach.

The permit system was a necessary evil, Arjun knew this. The money funded the Sagarmatha Pollution Control Committee, which hauled tonnes of trash off the slopes. It paid for rescue operations, for maintaining the trails teeming with trekkers. It was a large part of Nepal's tourism revenue. But as the numbers climbed higher each year, it felt less like conservation and more like exclusivity. The Himalayas were becoming a luxury resort for the global elite, a tick on a billionaire's bucket list.

Two weeks later, Arjun stood at Base Camp, a chaotic, colourful city of tents sprawled on the Khumbu icefall. The air was thin, sharp, and filled with the sounds of a dozen different languages. He watched Mark and his team, clad in their high-tech, brand-sponsored gear, doing acclimatization walks. They were good men, strong and determined. But they had purchased a service, and they expected a return on their investment.

Later that evening, Arjun sat in the mess tent with his team of Sherpas—Mingma, Dorje, and young Tenzing. They were reviewing the route through the icefall, a treacherous, shifting labyrinth of seracs and crevasses. Their skill would be the difference between life and death for the clients. Their fee, while substantial by Nepali standards, was a fraction of the permit cost.

"You know," Mingma said, stirring his tea, "my father carried loads for the Swiss in the 50s. He said they paid the village headman a goat and some rice for the right to climb." He smiled, his face a roadmap of high-altitude sun and wind. "Now, it costs more than a hundred goats."

They all laughed, but the truth of it hung in the air. The mountain's price had been transformed. It was no longer measured in livestock or rupees, but in dollars and ambition.

The summit push came on a rare, clear night. Arjun coordinated from Advanced Base Camp, his radio crackling with updates. He listened to Mingma's calm voice guiding Mark, who was struggling, his commercial oxygen running low. "Just a little more, Sahib. You are strong." It was a kindness, an encouragement that no permit fee could buy.

When the call finally came—"Summit! All team on summit!"—a cheer went up through the camp. Arjun felt a surge of relief and pride. But it was tempered by a quiet unease.

Later, as the exhausted but elated team descended, Mark gripped Arjun's shoulder, his eyes wide with the thrill of conquest. "We did it, Arjun! Worth every penny!"

Arjun nodded, forcing a smile. He looked past Mark, up at the majestic, indifferent peak, glowing in the evening sun. The clients had paid a fortune for the summit. His team had paid with their skill, their courage, their intimate knowledge of every deadly curve and fold of the mountain.

The real price of climbing the Himalayas, he realized, wasn't the number on the permit. That was just the cost of entry. The true cost was carried on the backs of the Sherpas, in the risk they took with each step through the icefall, in a relationship with the mountain that was being slowly commodified. The government had put a price on the altitude, but it could never put a price on the respect, the history, the soul. That was a currency that was becoming increasingly rare, thinning out like the air just below the summit, where only the most essential things could survive.
